<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Work song · Sacred · 1863 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Michael, Row the Boat Ashore</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A rowing song of freed people on an island in South Carolina, first written down during the American Civil War.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>formerly enslaved African Americans of the Sea Islands</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>St. Helena Island, South Carolina, United States</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>noted 1862–1863; first published 1867</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Work song · Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional spiritual, first published 1867 in Slave Songs of the United States)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-row-the-boat-ashore">Michael, Row the Boat Ashore</a>&quot; is a traditional spiritual first noted during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> on St. Helena Island, one of the Sea Islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>. It was sung by formerly enslaved people whose owners had abandoned the island before the Union navy arrived to enforce its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>. Charles Pickard Ware, an abolitionist and Harvard graduate who came to supervise the island's plantations from 1862 to 1865, wrote it down in music notation as he heard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">freedmen</a> sing it, and his cousin William Francis Allen reported in 1863 that they sang it as they rowed him across Station <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muscogee">Creek</a>. Allen, Ware and Lucy McKim Garrison first published it in 1867 in Slave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, in two versions: one in unrhymed couplets, one in rhyme.</p><p>Its words tell of crossing the River Jordan, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> was baptised, an image that can stand for deliverance and salvation, for the border of the Promised Land, and for death and the passage to Heaven. According to Allen, the Michael of the song is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arkhangelsk">Archangel</a> Michael, whom Catholic tradition often sees as the guide of the souls of the dead. The song stayed alive in the Sea Islands' oral tradition: Guy and Candie Carawan recorded it on Johns Island in the 1960s, and Janie Hunter of the Moving Star Hall singers recalled that her father, the son of formerly enslaved people, sang it as he rowed his boat back to shore after fishing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smithsonian">Smithsonian</a> Folkways released the Carawans' recording of Hunter and three Moving Star Hall singers in 1967.</p><p>The version most people know was adapted in 1954 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> folk singer and teacher Tony Saletan, who found the song in a library copy of Slave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. He kept its one-word chorus, added harmony and, to give teenagers rhymes, replaced the original verses, often a single line sung twice, with traditional African-American couplets. He taught it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a> the same year, the Weavers sang it at their Christmas Eve reunion concert of 1955, and Saletan shared a 1958 copyright in the adaptation with the members of the Weavers. The Highwaymen's version, titled simply &quot;Michael&quot;, reached No. 1 on the Billboard Hot 100 for two weeks in September 1961 and No. 1 in the United Kingdom; it changed the line about meeting the singer's mother on the other side to &quot;milk and honey&quot;, the Promised Land of Exodus. Lonnie Donegan, Harry Belafonte, Trini Lopez and, in French, Rika Zaraï recorded it soon after, and Seeger taught it to Big Bird on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sesame">Sesame</a> Street in 1970.</p><p>In September 1961, the month the Highwaymen's record reached No. 1, Herbert Lee, a charter member of the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naacp">NAACP</a>, was murdered in Amite County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>. According to a note smuggled out of the county jail by Bob <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, civil rights activists arrested for protesting the killing sang a version of &quot;Michael&quot; to keep their spirits up, led by Hollis Watkins.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A rowing song and a spiritual. The singers call on Michael to row the boat ashore, answering each line with &quot;Hallelujah&quot;, and the crossing of the river becomes the crossing of the Jordan into the Promised Land and the passage from death to Heaven. The mood is steady and hopeful, kept in time with the oars.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Michael row de boat ashore, Hallelujah!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Michael boat a gospel boat, Hallelujah!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I wonder where my mudder deh. [there]</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>See my mudder on de rock gwine home.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On de rock gwine home in Jesus' name.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Michael boat a music boat.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Gabriel blow de trumpet horn.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O you mind your boastin' talk.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Boastin' talk will sink your soul.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Brudder, lend a helpin' hand.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Sister, help for trim dat boat.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Jordan stream is wide and deep.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Jesus stand on t' oder side.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I wonder if my maussa deh.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>My fader gone to unknown land.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O de Lord he plant his garden deh.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He raise de fruit for you to eat.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>He dat eat shall neber die.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When de riber overflow.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O poor sinner, how you land?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Riber run and darkness comin'.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Sinner row to save your soul.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional; Slave Songs of the United States (1867), as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael%2C_Row_the_Boat_Ashore" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; in the public domain in the United States.</p></div></section>
</div>
